== STEP 5: PII-safety grep of the new AppLogger.failure call ==
Call site (loyalty_repository.dart:23-30) passes only:
  ApiFailure(statusCode), StackTrace.current,
  'point-to-wallet: non-map 200 body (${response.body.runtimeType})',
  endpoint: AppConstants.loyaltyPointTransferUri,
  status: statusCode,
  correlationId: ApiClient.requestIdFromResponse(response.headers)
No response.body, no statusText, no token. AppLogger.failure's own signature
(lib/helper/app_logger.dart:58-65) accepts only e/st/reason/endpoint/status/
correlationId — it exposes no parameter that could carry a raw body. The
new test's PII canary assertion ('SENTINEL2149' / '<html>' / 'example.test'
must never appear in the recorded error/reason/custom-keys payload) also
passed under STEP 1's GREEN run.

== STEP 6: non-200 else branch — read, not just tested ==
Diff shows the else branch (real non-200, e.g. 400/500) is byte-for-byte
unchanged: `responseModel = ResponseModel(false, response.statusText);`
still fires exactly as before, reached only when statusCode != 200. The new
branch is inserted as an `else if (statusCode == 200)` strictly between the
existing `if (statusCode == 200 && body is Map)` and the existing
`else`, so it can never intercept a genuine non-200. The suite's "control: a
real non-200..." test (STEP 1) independently confirms this: exactly one
ApiFailure(status: 400) recorded, matching ApiChecker's own pre-existing
[FAIL] choke-point, not a second/duplicate emission from the new branch.

